WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:03.920
Brady has started a new video channel, about words.

00:00:04.580 --> 00:00:06.260
One of the words is vodka,

00:00:07.180 --> 00:00:10.660
and he's challenged me to say something chemical about vodka.

00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:13.620
It's really too easy, but I'll do it all the same.

00:00:14.660 --> 00:00:15.940
The first thing, which...

00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:18.760
some of you may have seen on our other videos,

00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:22.260
is that Mendeleev, father of the periodic table,

00:00:22.340 --> 00:00:25.380
worked out the method of measuring

00:00:25.620 --> 00:00:29.400
the standard concentration of alcohol in vodka,

00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:31.180
at about 40%.

00:00:31.660 --> 00:00:35.040
So you can buy vodka with Mendeleev's name on it,

00:00:35.360 --> 00:00:37.480
and the periodic table.

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:40.720
And... I keep this bottle in my office,

00:00:41.260 --> 00:00:44.000
to drink when something sad happens.

00:00:44.420 --> 00:00:48.520
A friend dies, or... a grant is not funded.

00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:51.360
Here's a picture of Mendeleev on my tie.

00:00:51.620 --> 00:00:55.000
In some parts of Russia, he is more famous for vodka

00:00:55.100 --> 00:00:57.260
than he was for the periodic table.

00:00:57.420 --> 00:01:01.540
A few years ago, I found this bottle of vodka in France.

00:01:01.580 --> 00:01:04.300
It has "Poliakov", which is my surname,

00:01:04.380 --> 00:01:05.960
so I was quite excited.

00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:09.680
But... it's spelled with a V, rather than a double-F,

00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:12.060
the way I spell it, because...

00:01:13.160 --> 00:01:15.900
over the years, the way that people translate

00:01:15.900 --> 00:01:20.340
the Russian alphabet into English letters has changed.

00:01:20.480 --> 00:01:22.000
But it's the same name.

00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:24.780
Don't know if it's as good as the Mendeleev one.

00:01:25.660 --> 00:01:30.320
But the real scientific reason that I can talk about vodka

00:01:30.520 --> 00:01:33.860
is with this bottle, Stolichnaya.

00:01:33.860 --> 00:01:37.440
It means "capital vodka", which you can see is empty.

00:01:38.440 --> 00:01:41.180
And the reason it's empty is because

00:01:41.360 --> 00:01:44.460
the vodka in this bottle was used by one of my students,

00:01:45.080 --> 00:01:48.720
Sasha Novitskiy, in an experiment.

00:01:49.640 --> 00:01:54.440
We had invented a new way of measuring the phase behavior,

00:01:54.720 --> 00:01:56.740
that is, to find when you heat up

00:01:57.080 --> 00:01:59.520
a liquid, when it goes into a gas.

00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:02.700
And we measured- invented a way for measuring

00:02:02.700 --> 00:02:06.840
the phase behavior of mixtures of alcohol and water.

00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:10.760
And when we were preparing the paper to publish,

00:02:11.500 --> 00:02:15.580
Sasha said it'd be really nice to try a mixture of...

00:02:15.580 --> 00:02:18.680
a real mixture of alcohol and water, vodka.

00:02:19.120 --> 00:02:23.380
So... we did the experiment, and it worked,

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:27.580
and here you can see the scientific paper,

00:02:27.720 --> 00:02:32.900
and here is the phase diagram of Stolichnaya vodka,

00:02:32.900 --> 00:02:34.960
as a function of temperature and pressure.

00:02:34.960 --> 00:02:37.900
It worked really quite well, except...

00:02:38.580 --> 00:02:43.240
we forgot that vodka also contains a small amount of sugar.

00:02:43.480 --> 00:02:47.860
And at the high temperature, the sugar turned to caramel,

00:02:47.920 --> 00:02:49.440
and by the end of the experiment,

00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:51.160
the whole apparatus was blocked.

00:02:51.900 --> 00:02:56.660
I should just say that vodka's played quite a, um...

00:02:56.800 --> 00:02:58.020
role in my life.

00:02:58.340 --> 00:03:02.260
One of the first poems that I ever learnt in Russian

00:03:02.360 --> 00:03:03.820
was about vodka.

00:03:04.140 --> 00:03:06.800
It went, "Chizhik-Pyzhik, gde ty byl?

00:03:06.800 --> 00:03:08.740
"Na Fontanke vodku pil!

00:03:09.020 --> 00:03:10.860
"Vypil ryumku, vypil dve-

00:03:10.860 --> 00:03:12.660
"Zashumelo v golove."

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:16.200
"Little sparrow, little sparrow, where have you been?

00:03:16.700 --> 00:03:20.140
"I've been on the Fontanke," a river in Saint Petersburg,

00:03:21.260 --> 00:03:22.860
"and I was drinking vodka.

00:03:22.860 --> 00:03:25.920
"I drank one little glass, then another,

00:03:26.180 --> 00:03:28.560
"And now a noise has started in my head."

00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:31.000
And if you want to find out more about vodka,

00:03:31.000 --> 00:03:33.480
or samovar, or lots of other words,

00:03:33.560 --> 00:03:35.280
go and see Brady's website.

00:03:35.280 --> 00:03:38.580
It's quite fun. I've learnt quite a lot already.

00:03:38.800 --> 00:03:40.320
And I'm sure you will, too.

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:42.220
[Brady] Is it better than Periodic Table of Videos?

00:03:43.100 --> 00:03:46.660
Well, that, I would not want to be judgmental about my colleagues.

